<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Dryden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1631">1631</a>–<a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Glorious John</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Dryden was an English poet, literary critic, translator and playwright, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s first Poet Laureate, who so dominated the literary life of Restoration England that the period came to be known as the Age of Dryden. He welcomed the Restoration with Astraea Redux in 1660 and wrote satires such as Mac Flecknoe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalom">Absalom</a> and Achitophel (1681). The Hind and the Panther (1687) celebrates his conversion to Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. After the Glorious Revolution he refused the oaths of allegiance to William and Mary, lost his public offices and lived by his pen, translating The Works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> (1697). He died in 1700 and was reburied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-dryden.jpg" alt="John Dryden" width="330" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Dryden_portrait_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Translations carried it into the vernaculars: Gavin Douglas's Scots Eneados of 1513, <b>John Dryden</b>'s English version, partial versions by Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, and Seamus Heaney, and many modern verse translations, among them those of Cecil Day-Lewis, Robert Fitzgerald, Robert Fagles and Sarah Ruden.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Another English version, by the poet <b>John Dryden</b>, was first published in his Examen Poeticum (1693).</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
